WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.755 position:50% size:92% align:center
This teaching kernel follows one scalar query

00:00:03.755 --> 00:00:06.981 position:50% size:92% align:center
over eight keys and eight values. The keys and

00:00:06.981 --> 00:00:09.645 position:50% size:92% align:center
values are input arrays in GPU memory.

00:00:09.865 --> 00:00:12.439 position:50% size:92% align:center
The question is which intermediate values the

00:00:12.439 --> 00:00:15.071 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread must keep while it visits those arrays.

00:00:17.091 --> 00:00:19.874 position:50% size:92% align:center
With two keys per tile, the thread visits four

00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:22.596 position:50% size:92% align:center
tiles. It updates a running maximum, a sum of

00:00:22.596 --> 00:00:24.835 position:50% size:92% align:center
weights and a weighted sum of values.

00:00:25.055 --> 00:00:27.926 position:50% size:92% align:center
These are per-thread working values during SM

00:00:27.926 --> 00:00:30.670 position:50% size:92% align:center
execution, not full arrays of scores stored

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.265 position:50% size:92% align:center
between separate kernels.

00:00:34.285 --> 00:00:37.289 position:50% size:92% align:center
When a later tile increases the maximum, the old

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:40.293 position:50% size:92% align:center
sums are rescaled to that new maximum before new

00:00:40.293 --> 00:00:41.795 position:50% size:92% align:center
contributions are added.

00:00:42.015 --> 00:00:44.638 position:50% size:92% align:center
The first tile’s working state is shown here.

00:00:44.638 --> 00:00:46.154 position:50% size:92% align:center
The state survives so that

00:00:46.154 --> 00:00:47.903 position:50% size:92% align:center
earlier keys still contribute.

00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:53.621 position:50% size:92% align:center
After all eight keys, the weighted sum divided

00:00:53.621 --> 00:00:56.515 position:50% size:92% align:center
by the weight sum gives about 7.421.

00:00:56.735 --> 00:00:59.614 position:50% size:92% align:center
This scalar starter demonstrates the dependency

00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:01.820 position:50% size:92% align:center
and retained state; it is not a full

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.393 position:50% size:92% align:center
high-performance attention implementation.

00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:09.454 position:50% size:92% align:center
Now consider four keys per tile, with the same

00:01:09.454 --> 00:01:11.370 position:50% size:92% align:center
query, keys and values. Pause

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.155 position:50% size:92% align:center
and predict the tile count.

00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:15.841 position:50% size:92% align:center
Should visiting fewer tiles change which eight

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:17.449 position:50% size:92% align:center
keys contribute to the result?

00:01:23.269 --> 00:01:26.177 position:50% size:92% align:center
There are now two tiles. The same eight keys and

00:01:26.177 --> 00:01:28.116 position:50% size:92% align:center
values still contribute, and the

00:01:28.116 --> 00:01:29.691 position:50% size:92% align:center
illustrated result agrees.

00:01:29.911 --> 00:01:32.878 position:50% size:92% align:center
The memory benefit comes from retaining a small

00:01:32.878 --> 00:01:35.277 position:50% size:92% align:center
running state instead of materialising

00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:37.612 position:50% size:92% align:center
intermediate arrays. Tile count alone

00:01:37.612 --> 00:01:39.191 position:50% size:92% align:center
does not measure runtime.
